As the energy crisis and environmental issues have become more and more prominent due to the overuse of fossil fuels,[1-2] the design of efficient energy conversion and storage electrochemical systems like fuel cells, metal-air batteries and water electrolysis becomes highly urgent.[3-5] However, with sluggish kinetics due to the multistep proton-coupled electron transfer, oxygen evolution reaction (OER, $4\mathsf{O H}^{-}\to2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}+O_{2}+$ 4e- in base) has always limited the efficiency of the electrochemical systems.[6] Currently, the most cathodic OER catalysts are the precious metal-based materials,[7] which are however also require large overpotentials to reach the desirable current densities. In addition, due to the rarity and high-cost even the state-of-the-art catalysts cannot satisfy the scale-up applications yet.[8] Therefore, tremendous efforts have been devoted to develop highly efficient and stable OER catalysts via tuning the components and composition, such as metal oxides,[9] hydroxides,[10] chalcogenides,[11] nitrides,[12] phosphides[13] and the carbon-based materials.[14,15]
Benefiting from the high specific surface area and porosity, as well as the diversity in metal centers and functional groups, MOFs have aroused great interest in many fields.[16-17] In terms of electrocatalysis, they would be one of the best precursors for preparing the metal oxides/porous carbon materials via annealing, where numerous studies have demonstrated the promising performance of these MOFs-derived catalysts.[18] Notably, the pyrolysis process often causes the loss of the organic ligands and agglomeration of metals, which will destroy the structure completely and decrease the number of active sites. Nevertheless, from a structural point of view MOFs with the advantages of homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts for electrocatalysis[19-21] can be directly used as the OER catalysts without calcination. The coordinatively unsaturated metal atoms and hydroxide ligands on the surface, along with the bimetallic coupling effect could greatly increase the electrocatalytic OER activities,[22-24] while the design of functional MOFs that can be directly adopted as efficient OER catalysts remains challenge.
