MIL-n (Material of Institute Lavoisier) is one of the famous MOFs investigated extensively.[25] MIL-53(Fe) constructed by 1,4-bezenedicarboxylate (1,4-BDC) and $\mathsf{F e O}_{6}$ octahedrons has a flexible structure and one-dimensional (1D) channels for mass transport.[26] Apart from the interesting breathing effect, metal ions with high valence greatly improve the stability of MOFs[27] and the reversible redox reaction between $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ makes it possible to be a potential cathode material.[28] Owing to the fact that the $\mathsf{F e O}_{6}$ octahedrons can be partly replaced by $N i O_{6}$ ones,[29] herein we designed a facile strategy to synthesize a series of rod-shaped Fe/Ni bimetallic MOFs (Scheme 1), in which the molar ratios of Fe and Ni can be precisely tuned. These MOFs can be directly used for OER electrocatalysis. When introducing the third metal ions $\mathsf{\mathsf{(C o^{2+}}}$ or $\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}$ ) or using nickel foam (NF), the resulting trimetallic MOFs $({\mathsf{F e}}/{\mathsf{N i}}/{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{M n}})$ - MIL-53 and $F e/N i/C o(M n)\cdot M L\cdot53/N F)$ showed further enhanced OER performances as compared with the bimetallic MOFs, which are among the best OER electrocatalysts reported to date (Table S1).
Solvothermal reactions of $N i(N O_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O$ , $F e C l_{3}\cdot6H_{2}O$ and 1,4-BDC (molar ratio $=2.4{:}1{:}1$ ) at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $3h$ followed by drying at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12\mathsf{h}$ afforded bimetallic MOF, $\mathsf{F e/N i}_{2.4}$ -MIL-53. The field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM) and transmission electron microscope (TEM) images reveal that these MOFs are uniform rod-like nanocrystals with an average length of ca. $900~\mathsf{n m}$ and diameter of ca. $100~\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 1a-b and Figure S1). Since the molar ratio of metals can be easily tuned by changing the amount of the reactants for the bimetallic MOFs,[30] the present method can also be applied for the preparation of monometallic (Fe-MIL-53, Figure S2) as well as other bimetallic MOFs by readily changing the amount of $N i(N O_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O$ $\mathsf{F e/N i}_{1.6}$ -MIL-53 and Fe/Ni2.0-MIL-53, Figure S3) and the types of metal precursors (Fe/Mn-MOFs and Fe/CoMOFs, Figure S4). Their IR spectra are consistent with those of the reported MIL-53 (Fe) (Figure S5).[31] The PXRD patterns of these MOFs with different molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e/N i}$ have similar diffraction peaks (Figure 1c and Figure S6), which match those of the reported MIL-53 (Fe) and the simulated pattern.[32-33] Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) and X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) reveal that these nanocrystals consist of C, O, Fe and Ni (Figure S7-S8).[34] STEM-EDS mappings demonstrate that the element distributions of these four elements are highly uniform (Figure 1d).
Significantly, these bimetallic MOFs can be directly used for electrocatalytic OER without annealing treatment. Their electrocatalysis properties are evaluated with a typical threeelectrode system in 1.0 M KOH aqueous solution at room temperature. All the catalysts were dropped on a glassy carbon electrode (GC) with a loading of $0.510~\mathsf{m g}\cdot\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , which was used as the working electrode. The potential values are calibrated against the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). The linear sweep voltammetry curves of the bimetallic $F e/N i_{x}–M l L-53$ $({\sf x}=$ 1.6, 2.0, 2.4), pure GC and commercial Ir/C $(5\ \%\ |\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ ) are shown in Figure 2a. The pure GC has no OER activity. Compared with the commercial $\mathsf{I r/C}$ , the bimetallic MOFs $F e/N i_{x}{\mathrm{-}}M l L{\mathrm{-}}53/C$ ( $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=$ 1.6, 2.0, 2.4) exhibit excellent OER performances with the overpotentials of $258m\vee$ , $258~\mathsf{m V}$ , $244~\mathsf{m V}$ at the current density of 10 $\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , respectively, much smaller than that of Ir/C $(310~\mathsf{m V})$ . They also show lower Tafel slopes of 37.8 mV·dec-1, $45.5~\mathsf{m V}\cdot\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ , $48.7~\mathrm{\mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ than $\mathsf{I r/C}$ of 53.8 mV·dec-1 (Figure 2b).
By further introducing small amount of $C_{0}(N O_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O$ or $M n(N O_{3})_{2}.4H_{2}O$ , four different trimetallic MOFs $(F e/N i_{2.4}/M_{x}-M l L-$ 53 $(\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C o}$ , Mn; $\textsf{X}=\ 0.2,\ 0.4)$ ) can be successfully prepared (Figure 3a-c and Figure S9). The successful creation of these trimetallic MOFs have been also confirmed by means of PXRD, IR, XPS and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption (Figures S10-13). Significantly, these newly-created MOFs can exhibit further enhanced performance compared with bimetallic MOFs (Figure 3d). Among them, the optimized $\mathsf{F e}/\mathsf{N i}_{2.4}/\mathsf{C o}_{0.4}$ -MIL-53 has the outstanding OER performance, with the overpotential of $219\mathsf{m V}$ and $236~\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and $20\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , respectively. At a potential of $1.50\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE), it can deliver $79.6~\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ for the OER, two times higher than that of $\mathsf{F e/N i}_{2.4}$ -MIL-53 (33.6 mA·cm2) (Figure 3e), making it among the most active OER electrocatalysts reported to date (Table S1). The Tafel plot of as low as $53.5~\mathsf{m V}\cdot\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ further indicates that the kinetics of these trimetallic MOFs are quite satisfactory (Figure 3f). For trimetallic MOFs, while partial sites are active at relative low overpotential, more sites turn to active with the increasing of overpotential, which makes their slightly higher Tafel slopes than those of bimetallic MOFs.[35] In addition, the $\mathsf{F e}/\mathsf{N i}_{2.4}/\mathsf{C o}_{0.4}$ -MIL-53 also exhibits high durability, in which their OER polarization curve and morphology showed limited changes after 1000 potential cycles (Figure 3g, Figure S14).
In order to understand the reason for the OER activity enhancement observed with these trimetallic MOFs, the possible factors that might affect the activity were carefully explored. We first used the cyclic voltammogram (CV) method to calculate the double layer capacitance $\big(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d l}}\big)$ (Figure S15), which is one half to the electrode surface area (ECSA). The slope of the curves, representing the $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d}}$ in Figure S15c, reveals that these catalysts have very similar ECSAs, indicating that they have the similar active site numbers at the solid-liquid interface.
For the Fe/Ni-based catalysts, Ni is generally recognized as the active center for oxygen evolution,[36-37] and the incorporation of Fe could lead the ${|\mathsf{N i}^{3+}/\mathsf{N i}^{2+}}$ peak shifts to the higher potential, thus improves the OER performance.[38] Since both the $\mathsf{F e/N i}_{2.4^{-}}$ MIL-53 and $\mathsf{F e}/\mathsf{N i}_{2.4}/\mathsf{C o}_{0.4}$ -MIL-53 have a homogeneous distribution of active centers (Figure 1c and Figure 3b-c), the synergistic effect of metal-metal can exert crucial impact on improving the OER performance.[39], in which the Ni species are served as active sites to facilitate the diffusion and protoncoupled electron transfer of electrolytes, while the Fe, Co or Mn could tune the intrinsic properties of catalysts. In contrast, the monometallic Fe-MIL-53 shows much worse OER performance than that of $\mathsf{F e/N i}_{2.4}$ -MIL-53 (Figure S16). Meanwhile, the Fe/Mn(Co)-MOFs without Ni show low catalytic OER performance with the overpotential of $430~\mathrm{mV}$ and $340~\mathsf{m V}$ at 10 $\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ as well as poor stability (Figure S17). Thus Fe/Nibased MOFs are more suitable for OER catalysis in this system. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was further carried out to understand the impact of mixed metals on OER catalysis (Figure 4). The Ni 2p region for these MOFs shows two typical peaks around 856 eV and 874 eV that are associated with Ni $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2p_{1/2}$ electronic configurations, respectively. With the increase of the Ni content, the high-resolution Ni 2p spectrum shifts to higher binding energy, indicating modified local electronic structure of Ni. Compared with the bimetallic MOFs, the shift to higher binding energy in trimetallic MOFs is more significant (ca. $0.4\:\mathrm{eV})$ , which can be originate from a change in the coordination environment of the active centers caused by introducing the Co and is crucial in modulating electronic properties of the active centers and thus promoting the OER catalysis.[40] Because of the synergy effect of the mixed metals, the efficient modulating electronic environment of the active metal sites is likely favorable for improving the catalytic OER.[41] Therefore, the optimized active Ni sites modulated by the other metals play dominant role for the enhanced OER activity.
The potential applications of electrocatalysts are limited by using the glassy electrode as the electrode, while the porous nickel foam (NF) with high conductivity is recognized as one of the best substrate.[41] Significantly, these highly efficient catalysts can be readily grown on the NF (Figure S18), in which these MOFs are not simply loaded but anchored on the NF surface (Figure S19). While the pure NF has negligible OER activity (Figure 5a, dark line), the bimetallic Fe/Ni-MOFs grown on NF shows excellent OER performance with the overpotential of 300 and $370~\mathsf{m V}$ at the current density of 100 and 500 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , respectively. When the third metal was introduced, the resulting catalysts possess improved OER performance compared with the bimetallic ones, where the optimized Fe/Ni/Mn0.4-MIL-53/NF exhibits the most outstanding OER performance (Figure 5a), which requires only overpotential of 238 and $290~\mathsf{m V}$ to achieve the current density of 100 and 500 $\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , respectively, with the corresponding Tafel slope of $71.3~\mathsf{m V}$ ·dec (Figure 5b). A prolonged chronopotentiometry test at the overpotential of $300~\mathrm{{mV}}$ vs. RHE was further conducted for $\mathsf{F e/N i/M n}_{0.4}$ -MIL-53/NF (Figure 5c), where only $6.7\%$ current density decay and limited morphology change was observed after $60\mathsf{h}$ (Figure S20).
To summarize, a series of Fe/Ni-based MOFs with modulated molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e/N i}$ have been successfully created, which can be directly adopted as efficient OER catalysts with high activity and stability. The performance of Fe/Ni-based MOFs can be further improved by forming trimetallic MOFs. The optimized $\mathsf{F e}/\mathsf{N i}_{2.4}/\mathsf{M n}_{0.4}$ -MIL-53 achieved the current density of $20\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at low overpotential of $236~\mathsf{m V}$ with a small Tafel slope of 52.2 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}{\cdot}\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ . The high performance can be attributed to the unique structure and porosity, as well as the synergy effect of the mixed metals. Both the OER activity and stability can be further enhanced by constructing the trimetallic MOFs on NF via selftemplate method. We anticipate that the present study will stimulate extensive explorations of novel MOFs with active sites and high stability for potential electrocatalysis.
